+    requires D >=Int 0 andBool D <=Int 9
+    [priority(40)]
+endmodule

codex
The direct failure was not a counterexample: the supplied `valSeqAt` is intentionally total-but-opaque for a modular symbolic index, so the prover stops before choosing an element. I’m addressing that semantics boundary with finite-domain lookup lemmas. Each lemma forces exhaustive cases for the only legal digit indices (2 cases for thousands, 10 for each other place), proves those cases using the original semantics, and only then supplies the generalized lookup equation to the main proof.
apply patch
patch: completed
/workspace/spec.k
/workspace/verification.k
diff --git a/solution.py b/solution.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..b44b22d6199b2e457122ccaf1e64a7cb0e993d21
--- /dev/null
+++ b/solution.py
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+def int_to_mini_roman(number):
+    thousands = ("", "m")
+    hundreds = ("", "c", "cc", "ccc", "cd", "d", "dc", "dcc", "dccc", "cm")
+    tens = ("", "x", "xx", "xxx", "xl", "l", "lx", "lxx", "lxxx", "xc")
+    ones = ("", "i", "ii", "iii", "iv", "v", "vi", "vii", "viii", "ix")
+
+    return (
+        thousands[number // 1000]
+        + hundreds[(number % 1000) // 100]
+        + tens[(number % 100) // 10]
+        + ones[number % 10]
+    )
